  <static seed="542" source="Library/FortLewis/Calc3/16-2-Iterated-integrals/HGM4-16-2-43-Iterated-integrals.pg">
    <statement><p>Set up a double integral in rectangular coordinates
for calculating the volume 
of the solid under the graph of the function 
<m>f(x,y) = 16-x^{2}-y^{2}</m> and above the plane <m>z = 7</m>.</p>
<p><em>Instructions:</em>
Please enter the integrand in the first answer box. 
Depending on the order of integration you choose,  
enter <em>dx</em> and <em>dy</em> 
in either order into the second and third answer boxes 
with only one <em>dx</em> or 
<em>dy</em> in each box.  Then, enter the limits of 
integration.</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle \int_A^B \int_C^D</m> 
<fillin characters="40" name="AnSwEr0001"/>
<fillin characters="5" name="MuLtIaNsWeR_AnSwEr0001_1"/>
<fillin characters="5" name="MuLtIaNsWeR_AnSwEr0001_2"/></p>
<p>A = <fillin characters="20" name="MuLtIaNsWeR_AnSwEr0001_3"/></p>
<p>B = <fillin characters="20" name="MuLtIaNsWeR_AnSwEr0001_4"/></p>
<p>C = <fillin characters="20" name="MuLtIaNsWeR_AnSwEr0001_5"/></p>
<p>D = <fillin characters="20" name="MuLtIaNsWeR_AnSwEr0001_6"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>9-x^{2}-y^{2};\,dx;\,dy;\,-3;\,3;\,-\sqrt{9-y^{2}};\,\sqrt{9-y^{2}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>SOLUTION</p>
<p>The function <m>f(x,y) = 16-x^{2}-y^{2}</m> intersects the plane <m>z = 7</m> when
<m>x^2 + y^2 = 9</m>.</p>
<p>Thus the region of integration is</p>
<p><m>\begin{array}{lcl}  D \amp =\amp  \left \lbrace (x,y) \, | \, -3 \le x  \le 3, 
-\sqrt{9-x^2} \le y \le \sqrt{9-x^2} \right\rbrace  \\
\amp =\amp  \left \lbrace (x,y) \, | \, -3 \le y  \le 3,
-\sqrt{9-y^2} \le x \le \sqrt{9-y^2} \right\rbrace  \end{array}</m>.</p>
<p>The volume is then</p>
<p><m>V= \int_{-3}^{3} \int_{-\sqrt{9-x^2}}^{\sqrt{9-x^2}} \left( 16-x^2-y^2 - 7
\right) \, dy \, dx</m> or</p>
<p><m>V= \int_{-3}^{3} \int_{-\sqrt{9-y^2}}^{\sqrt{9-y^2}} \left( 16-x^2-y^2 - 7
\right) \, dx \, dy</m>.</p>
</solution>
